 ## Overview
-This method implements a PyTorch Autograd Function that wraps the ray marching process, allowing gradients to flow through the depth prediction to the decoder parameters and latent code [S1, unofficial].
+A PyTorch `autograd.Function` that wraps the ray marching process to enable gradient flow from the predicted depth values back to the neural decoder parameters and latent codes [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard differentiable volume rendering by operating on the refined scalar depth function values (occupancy logits) at the predicted surface locations, using the chain rule through the implicit surface function rather than through the full volumetric integration [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-- `ray0`: Ray origins, shape $(N, 3)$.
-- `ray_direction`: Unit direction vectors, shape $(N, 3)$.
-- `decoder`: Neural network function $f_\phi(p, c)$ evaluating occupancy logits.
-- `c`: Latent codes, shape $(N, D)$.
-- `n_steps`: Number of ray marching steps.
-- `n_secant_steps`: Number of secant refinement steps.
-- `tau`: Temperature parameter for soft thresholding.
-- `depth_range`: Tuple $(d_{min}, d_{max})$ defining the search interval.
-- `method`: String specifying the intersection/refinement method.
-- `check_cube_intersection`: Boolean flag for boundary checks.
-- `max_points`: Maximum number of points to store.
-- `grad_output`: Gradient from the loss function w.r.t. the output depth, shape $(N, 1)$.
+**Inputs and Outputs**
+*   **Inputs**: `ray0` (origin, shape $[B, 3]$), `ray_direction` (unit direction, shape $[B, 3]$), `decoder` (neural network module), `c` (latent code, shape $[B, D]$), `d_pred` (predicted depths, shape $[B]$), `p_pred` (predicted 3D points, shape $[B, 3]$), `mask` (validity mask, shape $[B]$), `grad_output` (upstream gradient, shape $[B]$).
+*   **Outputs**: `grad_decoder_params`, `grad_c`.
 
-**Outputs:**
